ickiest part about beaufort is the  technique  that doesn't look right what did i do  wrong again  the dough you got to scoop it up kind of  swirl it in your hand and plop it  you just got to plop it right into the  hot oil  you got to get it formed into these nice  round balls that's how we typically eat  it  and it's not easy but i hope that  joelle is able to do it and that she  likes it okay i finished my breakfast  and i am so  excited for these they smell incredible  making these definitely takes practice  like first couple tries was rough but  then after a while they started getting  rounder  oh look at that  danielle's gonna try some oh it's good  right yeah daniel never likes my cooking  i mean i would totally make these and  bring them to like a brunch or a party  or something  and like serve these for people just  kind of have this finger food and it's  super good  alrighty for lunch for elise i chose to  have her make tendency  which is one of my favorite korean soups  it's  actually really similar to miso soup but  the soybean paste that you use as the  base of the broth is fermented so it has  a really strong rich flavor  can smell a little funky sometimes which  i think scares people off but the flavor  is incredible and so umami and you can  add really whatever you want to it  i like to add a lot of veggies some tofu  and  just you know all those fixings like  zucchini mushrooms whatever you might  have  it is super rich and i always have it  with some rice because it's quite salty  and so when you mix the rice in it's  just like so satisfying and the perfect  balance a lot of people think of kimchi  jjigae as being like the og  korean soup but i feel like tenjangtike  is  extremely underrated and it is so easy  to make and  incredibly satisfying so this is the  tanjong jigae yes  this is a soup i've never had fermented  bean paste before  okay very very very  umami type flavor like it's very deep  it's a deep deep flavor it's deep  it was pretty easy to follow the steps  like soup is always easy you know soup  is always pretty easy and this one's  really good  yeah okay so the next dish that i'm  having joelle make for lunch  is freaking jalof rice  with asada plantains jaloff rice is a  staple  gummy and dish and very very popular  throughout all of west africa ghana  jalaprice is basically made up of  jasmine rice and some tomato mixture  tomatoes seasonings habanero peppers  different things all combined together  you cook the rice in the tomato mixture  and voila you got jollof rice notice  that something was smelling  a little a little burnt and though the  top of my rice  might look you know nice and ricey dig a  little deeper  and you can see the bottom it's like  entirely charred  rice in a pot it's hard guys okay it is  it is quite difficult  to get the perfect consistency of rice  you can eat it with meat on the side  like chicken beef fish but another great  side dish  is plantains and specifically kilo la is  what i'm having joelle make today  kellowelle is ripe plantains chopped  into little pieces  seasoned with salt and crushed red  pepper and other things if you want  and it's fried in oil and it's like  super crispy  on the outside but the inside is so  sweet and chewy  it's to die for besides being incredibly  embarrassed by my lack of culinary  skills and ability to make rice which is  a really fundamental um  dish of my culture as well because we're  babied  with rice cookers i'm really excited to  try my julep rice and kelly welly i  think it'll taste  really good despite it looking a little  burnt it's despite everything looking a  little burnt my plantains looked a  little burnt too but  the rice is like very  very savory with all the different  spices i love the kick  of the hot banana pepper the texture of  my rice absolutely questionable  i know there will be many people out  there flaming me for not knowing how to  make rice in a pot  hmm like the natural flavor of plantain  brought 